WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.450
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:08.900
It's been quite a while since we've talked
about a paper on fluid simulations.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:14.280
Since my withdrawal symptoms are already kicking
in, today, I simply have to talk to you about

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:21.040
this amazing paint simulator program that
runs in real time and on our mobile devices.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.580
These handheld devices typically have a lower
image resolution compared to desktop computers,

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.410
therefore it is indeed a challenge to put
together a solution that artists can use to

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.610
create detailed paintings with.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:38.899
And to accomplish this, this piece of work
offers several killer features: for instance,

00:00:38.899 --> 00:00:42.140
the paint pigment concentration can be adjusted.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:45.309
The direction of the brush strokes is also
controllable.

00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:50.569
And third, this technique is powered by a
viscoelastic shallow water simulator that

00:00:50.569 --> 00:00:54.090
also supports simulating multiple layers of
paint.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:59.120
This is particularly challenging as the inner
paint layers may have already dried when adding

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.170
a new wet layer on top of them.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:05.140
This all has to be simulated in a way that
is physically plausible.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.590
But we're not done yet!

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:11.469
With many different kinds of paint types that
we're using, the overall outlook of our paintings

00:01:11.469 --> 00:01:15.420
are dramatically different depending on the
lighting conditions around them.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.320
To take this effect into consideration, this
technique also has an intuitive feature where

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:26.369
the effect of virtual light sources is also
simulated, and the output is changed interactively

00:01:26.369 --> 00:01:28.479
as we tilt the tablet around.

00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:34.710
And get this, gravity is also simulated, and
the paint trickles down depending on the orientation

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.780
of our tablet according to the laws of physics.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:38.829
Really cool!

00:01:38.829 --> 00:01:43.100
The paper also shows visual comparisons against
similar algorithms.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:48.950
And clearly, artists who work with these substances
all day know exactly how they should behave

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:53.869
in reality, so the ultimate challenge is always
to give it to them and ask them whether they

00:01:53.869 --> 00:01:58.249
have enjoyed the workflow and found the simulation
faithful to reality.

00:01:58.249 --> 00:02:00.310
Let the artists be the judge!

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:05.530
The user study presented in the paper revealed
that the artists loved the user experience

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:08.910
and they expressed that it's second to none
for testing ideas.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:13.890
I am sure that with a few improvements, this
could be the ultimate tool for artists to

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:19.220
unleash their creative potential while sitting
outside and getting inspired by nature.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:23.200
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

